WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.138
In this tutorial, we’ll turn meeting notes into a reviewed follow-up.

2
00:00:04.318 --> 00:00:10.223
We’re using a prepared demo workspace, fictional customer data, the Fixture
model, and simulated delivery.

3
00:00:13.283 --> 00:00:15.601
Start by checking the workspace mode.

4
00:00:15.781 --> 00:00:22.715
Reasoning and extraction both use the deterministic Fixture model here, so
this walkthrough makes no external model calls.

5
00:00:22.895 --> 00:00:25.040
Delivery is also simulated.

6
00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:29.033
Real integrations require separate administrator configuration.

7
00:00:30.417 --> 00:00:33.211
Open Customer workspace and choose New customer.

8
00:00:33.391 --> 00:00:38.847
Enter the company, industry, contact, and email, then select the responsible
team member.

9
00:00:39.027 --> 00:00:42.012
We’ll use a fictional customer called Northstar Studio.

10
00:00:42.192 --> 00:00:44.635
Create the record to open its workspace.

11
00:00:47.483 --> 00:00:50.247
Choose Add evidence and select Meeting notes.

12
00:00:50.427 --> 00:00:54.554
Give the record a useful title, then paste the customer’s original words.

13
00:00:54.734 --> 00:01:02.008
These notes say that customer records need to be unified, the budget is
still unapproved, and the team wants to review a plan by Friday.

14
00:01:02.188 --> 00:01:03.969
Save to begin processing.

15
00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:09.498
Scroll to customer facts.

16
00:01:09.678 --> 00:01:12.175
Each candidate retains its source quote.

17
00:01:12.355 --> 00:01:17.336
Check the budget statement against the original notes, then confirm it with
a verification note.

18
00:01:17.516 --> 00:01:21.605
Confirming this fact does not approve a budget or change a deal outcome.

19
00:01:26.117 --> 00:01:29.781
Select Ask Intent and create a conversation for this customer.

20
00:01:29.961 --> 00:01:32.459
Ask what you should review before following up.

21
00:01:32.639 --> 00:01:38.866
The answer stays connected to the authorized customer context, with
supporting evidence and links to relevant work.

22
00:01:39.046 --> 00:01:41.478
This conversation is private to its creator.

23
00:01:43.717 --> 00:01:46.384
Return to the customer and open the draft.

24
00:01:46.564 --> 00:01:50.349
Review the recipient, subject, message, and original sources.

25
00:01:50.529 --> 00:01:53.291
Choose Edit draft if something needs changing.

26
00:01:53.471 --> 00:01:55.511
Saving creates a new revision.

27
00:01:55.691 --> 00:01:59.954
The version shown here is the exact version an authorized reviewer will
approve.

28
00:02:02.983 --> 00:02:06.123
When the content is ready, approve the displayed revision.

29
00:02:06.303 --> 00:02:09.321
The worker processes the action and records the result.

30
00:02:09.501 --> 00:02:14.701
Here, the receipt explicitly says simulated delivery completed: no real
email was sent.

31
00:02:14.881 --> 00:02:18.697
The receipt reference and operation ID make this outcome traceable.

32
00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:25.525
The customer record now connects the original notes, reviewed facts, action,
and receipt.

33
00:02:25.705 --> 00:02:28.269
A receipt proves delivery status only.

34
00:02:28.449 --> 00:02:33.317
When the customer replies, add that reply as new evidence and continue the
review cycle.

35
00:02:33.497 --> 00:02:36.577
Contracts and payments remain separate business records.

36
00:02:39.917 --> 00:02:42.915
You’ve completed your first evidence-to-action workflow.

37
00:02:43.095 --> 00:02:48.300
Explore the product guide below, or follow the deployment guide to prepare
your own workspace.

38
00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:51.817
Intent is open source under Apache 2.0.
